ns \"true story\"","catalog text contains \"story of how\""]},{"id":"biographical","score":0.612,"confidence":"medium","evidence":["catalog text contains \"family\"","catalog text contains \"families\"","biography subject or topic"]},{"id":"character_driven","score":0.34,"confidence":"low","evidence":["catalog text contains \"family\"","catalog text contains \"families\""]}],"method":"deterministic_catalog_heuristics_v1"},"experimentalSemanticProfile":{"centralFigures":[{"name":"Rachel Clarke","confidence":0.84}],"centralPlaces":[],"argument":{"present":false,"statement":"","confidence":0},"academicOrientation":{"score":30,"confidence":0.45},"profileConfidence":0.63,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralFigures":[{"name":"Keira Ball","confidence":0.62},{"name":"Max Johnson","confidence":0.62}]},"model":"gpt-5.4-nano-2026-03-17","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"c2104a53c3db41da767ebc18697534c6be900ddfa1f5452437de5fef52ed1b5d","reviewStatus":"flagged","validationWarnings":["author_included_for_review: Rachel Clarke"]},"summary":"Winner of the Women’s Prize for Nonfiction | Shortlisted for the Baillie Gifford Prize An unforgettable and inspiring true story of how one family’s grief transformed into a lifesaving gift—written by a bestselling author and palliative care doctor. The first of our organs to form and the last to die, the heart is both a simple pump and the symbol of what makes us human; as long as it continues to beat, there is hope. In The Story of a Heart, Dr. Rachel Clarke blends the history of medical innovations behind transplant surgery with the story of two children—one of whom desperately needs a new heart. One summer day, nine-year-old Keira Ball was in a terrible car accident and suffered catastrophic brain injuries. As the rest of her body began to shut down, her heart continued to beat. In an act of extraordinary generosity, Keira’s parents and siblings immediately agreed that she would have wanted to be an organ donor. Meanwhile nine-year-old Max Johnson had been in a hospital for nearly a year, valiantly fighting the virus that was causing his young heart to fail. When Max’s parents received the call they had been hoping for, they knew it came at a terrible cost to another family—in what Clarke calls “the brutal arithmetic of transplant surgery.” The act of Keira’s heart resuming its rhythm inside Max’s body was a medical miracle. But this was only part of the story.","pageCount":256,"publicationYear":2024,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","label":"Biography & Autobiography","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-the-story-of-a-heart-two-families-one-heart-and-a-medical-miracle-rachel-clarke"}],"primarySubject":"Memoir & Autobiography","secondarySubjects":["Society & Culture","Biography"],"confidence":"medium","rationale":"The book appears to center on personal experiences relating to health and medicine, which aligns with Medicine & Public Health, but also touches on societal aspects and personal narratives, making it relevant to Society & Culture and Biography.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"e03996d81975ce6d43c04360d75352aa04eef61140bed9e8d63b40a378504e5f","displaySummary":"The first of our organs to form and the last to die, the heart is both a simple pump and the symbol of what makes us human; as long as it continues to beat, there is hope. In The Story of a Heart, Dr. Rachel Clarke blends the history of medical innovations behind transplant surgery with the story of two children—one of whom desperately needs a new heart. One summer day, nine-year-old Keira Ball was in a terrible car accident and suffered catastrophic brain injuries. As the rest of her body began to shut down, her heart continued to beat. In an act of extraordinary generosity, Keira’s parents and siblings immediately agreed that she would have wanted to be an organ donor. Meanwhile nine-year-old Max Johnson had been in a hospital for nearly a year